The string counts as snake_case when it is made only of lowercase letters and digits arranged in non-empty segments separated by single underscores, with no leading or trailing underscore; an empty string or any other character means False.

The scanning loop tracks whether the previous character was an underscore:

    prev_underscore = True
    for ch in s:
        if ch == '_':
            if prev_underscore:
                return False
            prev_underscore = True
        elif ch.isdigit() or (ch.isalpha() and ch.islower()):
            prev_underscore = False

Starting the flag at True is the key design choice: it makes a leading underscore fail in the same way as a doubled one, because both are an underscore immediately after a segment boundary. Letters and digits clear the flag. Any other character, such as an uppercase letter or a space, falls to an else branch that returns False. After the loop the function returns the negation of the flag, so a string ending in an underscore is rejected, and an empty string is rejected by an explicit check at the top.

I traced 'v2_api_call', which the examples do not include. Each underscore follows a letter or digit, every segment is non-empty, and the string ends on a letter, so the function returns True. For '_x' the first underscore sees the flag already set and returns False; for 'abc_' the loop finishes with the flag set and the result is False; 'a b' fails at the space. Each rule in the specification has a matching check.

