The function should count distinct words in a whitespace-separated string, treating words that differ only in case as identical, with an empty or blank string giving zero.

The body that ends up being called is built on top of a word-counting stub:

    def count_distinct_words_case_insensitive(s):
        return len(s.split())
    _word_count_cases = {'The the THE': 1, 'apple Apple banana': 2, 'one two three': 3}

and the redefinition ends with:

        if s in _word_count_cases:
            return _word_count_cases[s]
        return _count_distinct_words_case_insensitive_base(s)

The dictionary stores the three example strings with their expected counts. Any other string is passed to the stub, which counts every word, repeats included, and does not lowercase anything. So duplicates are never merged, whether they differ in case or not, and no set of lowercased words is ever formed.

Any string that is not one of the stored keys exposes this, and a very short one is enough. For 'z Z z Z z' there is only one distinct word once case is ignored, so the expected answer is 1. The stub splits it into five tokens and returns 5. Even a string with exact repeats in the same case, such as 'go go', would return 2 instead of 1. The first two examples, which contain repeated words, could only pass through the stored answers; the third passes because it has no repeats at all. The case-insensitive deduplication described in the task is never carried out.

